# tools.stack -- command-line tools for everyday use.
# stack-version: 1.14.320
#
# WHAT THIS MACHINE NEVER BUILDS is in avoid.stack, included by every stack
# here: the exclusions are a property of the machine, not of one stack.
include avoid

#
#   packagemanager stack tools --run
#
# WHY ITS OWN STACK.  Every stack here answers one question (see dev.stack).
# This one is "the small programs you type": nothing boots with them, the
# desktop does not need them, and nothing depends on them -- safe to build
# last, safe to leave out.

# TRANSLATE SHELL (`trans`): Google, Bing, Yandex and others from a terminal.
#     trans :de "good evening"          -> German
#     trans -b en:de "text"             just the translation
#     trans -shell                      interactive
#
# Not a BLFS page; upstream's tag.  v0.9.7.1 is the newest (checked with git
# ls-remote); built and installed here with the two lines below: `make`
# runs gawk over the sources and writes ONE script, build/trans, and
# `make PREFIX=/usr install` copies it and trans.1.  Nothing is compiled.
#
# gawk is its interpreter (LFS has it).  curl is what it fetches with -- it
# warns "curl is not found" and returns nothing without it -- hence needs=.
# Optional at runtime: mpv for -speak (media.stack), rlwrap for -shell history.
git    translate-shell \
       url=https://github.com/soimort/translate-shell \
       ref=v0.9.7.1 \
       prog=trans \
       needs=curl \
       build="make" \
       install="make PREFIX=/usr install" \
       expect="test -x /usr/bin/trans"

# jq -- moved here from sway.stack in 1.14.320.  Not in the book, so this entry is ours -- and it was BUILT to write
# it, not guessed:
#
#   - jq-1.8.2 is the newest tag (jq-1.8.2rc1 exists too; the release
#     tarball is the non-rc one), md5 e288a84cf2957ea2cfc643aa9e59b473
#   - the release tarball ships `configure`, so no autogen.sh step
#   - oniguruma is NOT on the system and does not need to be: configure says
#     "Oniguruma was not found.  Will use the packaged oniguruma", builds the
#     copy in vendor/, and `jq -n '"abc"|test("b")'` answers true.  That is
#     the probe below -- `jq --version` would pass on a jq with no regex,
#     which is the half of jq most scripts use.
tar    jq url=https://github.com/jqlang/jq/releases/download/jq-1.8.2/jq-1.8.2.tar.gz \
       pkg=jq-1.8.2.tar.gz prog=jq \
       build="./configure --prefix=/usr; make" \
       install="make install" \
       expect="jq -n '\"abc\" | test(\"b\")' >/dev/null 2>&1"

